# Vigil — configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local (that is the file cmd/vigil-server reads, not .env):
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every value below has a working default. Vigil runs with none of them set:
# intent policy, cost forecasting, and the behavioral detectors all work with
# no credentials of any kind. The optional sections buy you AI judgement,
# trace export, and persistence.
#
# Never commit a filled-in copy. .gitignore already excludes .env* .

# ─── Core ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
VIGIL_ADDR=":8080"
VIGIL_ORG_ID="default"
# Must be the externally reachable URL in production — OAuth discovery
# advertises it to Claude Web.
VIGIL_PUBLIC_BASE="http://localhost:8080"
VIGIL_DASHBOARD_BASE="http://localhost:3000"

# ─── Cost firewall ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-session spend ceiling in USD. Sessions arriving outside the OAuth consent
# flow (which carries its own approved budget) get this.
VIGIL_BUDGET_LIMIT="100"
# Fraction of budget at which Vigil recommends a cheaper model route instead of
# blocking. The hard limit is the budget itself.
# VIGIL_SOFT_LIMIT_PCT="0.80"

# ─── Operator control token ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Guards the endpoints that kill, pause, resume, block, or re-budget an agent.
# Unset, those endpoints are OPEN and the server warns loudly at startup — which
# keeps `make demo` working with zero setup. Set this before binding anywhere
# but loopback: an unauthenticated kill switch is worse than no kill switch.
VIGIL_CONTROL_TOKEN=""

# ─── Shell execution ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# run_command executes `bash -c` on caller-supplied input. It is DISABLED by
# default and must be turned on deliberately. Do not enable it on a
# host reachable from an untrusted network without also adding a command
# allowlist, a restricted working directory, and OS-level resource limits.
VIGIL_ALLOW_EXEC="false"

# ─── Audit ledger ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Append-only SHA-256 hash chain of every governance decision.
# Verify with: vigil-cli audit verify [session_id]
VIGIL_AUDIT_PATH="./vigil-audit.jsonl"

# ─── Inference (OPTIONAL) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without any key below, Vigil runs deterministic-only: no model is consulted
# and no risk score is produced. That is a supported configuration, not a
# degraded one — it is never faked with a synthesized verdict.
#
# Three real vendors ship in priority order (pkg/query-service/vigil/llm/chain.go):
# Featherless, then NVIDIA, then Gemini. Chain.Complete tries each configured
# one in order and only moves past a vendor once it actually refuses (expired
# credit, revoked key) — so a deployment with only NVIDIA and Gemini keys
# still runs the full product, not a degraded one, and adding a Featherless
# key later slots it back in first with no code change.
#
# Featherless is the hackathon's compute partner, speaking the OpenAI
# /chat/completions wire format like every vendor below:
# VIGIL_FEATHERLESS_API_KEY=""
# VIGIL_FEATHERLESS_BASE_URL="https://api.featherless.ai/v1"
#
# Model IDs have NO defaults on purpose. Catalogues change fast enough that a
# hardcoded ID retires mid-development — this exact repo hit that: a Groq
# model used for local testing was pulled from the catalogue within the same
# session it was configured. A role with no model is simply inactive.
#
# Current as of Aug 2026 — verify against https://featherless.ai/models before
# relying on them, per the note above:
# VIGIL_FEATHERLESS_MODEL_FAST="moonshotai/Kimi-K3"
# VIGIL_FEATHERLESS_MODEL_REASONER="moonshotai/Kimi-K3"
# VIGIL_FEATHERLESS_MODEL_REVIEWER="zai-org/GLM-5.2"
#
# NVIDIA NIM (build.nvidia.com). Verify each model ID with a real completion
# before relying on it — /v1/models lists models this account may not
# actually be able to invoke; two catalogue entries 404'd in this build's
# testing despite being listed (see README, "Three real shipped vendors").
# VIGIL_NVIDIA_API_KEY=""
# VIGIL_NVIDIA_BASE_URL="https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1"
# VIGIL_NVIDIA_MODEL_FAST="meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct"
# VIGIL_NVIDIA_MODEL_REASONER="nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1"
# VIGIL_NVIDIA_MODEL_REVIEWER="nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b"
#
# Gemini, via Google's OpenAI-compatibility layer. A free-tier key blocks
# "-pro" models outright (429, stated limit of 0 requests/day) and plain
# "-flash" models spend hidden "thinking" tokens before any visible content —
# "-flash-lite" has neither problem; verify with a real completion on your
# own key/tier before assuming a bigger model works.
# VIGIL_GEMINI_API_KEY=""
# VIGIL_GEMINI_BASE_URL="https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai"
# VIGIL_GEMINI_MODEL_FAST="gemini-3.5-flash-lite"
# VIGIL_GEMINI_MODEL_REASONER="gemini-3.5-flash-lite"
# VIGIL_GEMINI_MODEL_REVIEWER="gemini-3.5-flash-lite"
#
# VIGIL_MODEL_FAST / _REASONER / _REVIEWER (no vendor prefix) also work, and
# exist for local testing against a second OpenAI-compatible endpoint without
# touching the vendor table in pkg/query-service/vigil/llm/chain.go — see
# TestLiveVendors in llm/live_test.go, and TestLiveChainFromEnv for a proof
# against the real ChainFromEnv constructor the server uses.
#
# VIGIL_LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS="8"

# ─── HydraDB graph layer (OPTIONAL) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Without a key here, the firewall runs without the graph consult — intent,
# cost, and behavior checks still govern every call, same posture as Featherless
# being unconfigured. With one, HydraDB is consulted *before* Featherless: an
# uncertain intent verdict, an install/exec-shaped call, or a raised behavioral
# signal all ask the graph first, and Featherless only gets a turn if the graph
# itself had nothing.
# VIGIL_HYDRADB_API_KEY=""
# VIGIL_HYDRADB_DATABASE="vigil-os"
# VIGIL_HYDRADB_BASE_URL="https://api.hydradb.com"
#
# After setting the key, run once to populate the demo graph (real ingest, not
# fixtures — HydraDB extracts the entities/relationships itself):
#   vigil-cli hydra-seed --wait
# A HydraDB query is bounded by its own fixed 8s timeout (hydraTimeout in
# firewall/hydra.go), separate from VIGIL_JUDGE_BUDGET_SECONDS above, which
# only bounds the Featherless escalation stage. Real measured latency, by
# query shape: plain memory retrieval (graph_context=false) ~220-260ms,
# close to HydraDB's own published sub-200ms claim; graph_context=true
# structural queries (blast radius, maintainer graph, typosquats) 375ms-1s
# isolated, up to ~4-5s under concurrent load — see README for why the
# graph consult is not on every call, only on calls that already need one.
#
# Supply-chain incident response: a comma-separated denylist of "name" or
# "name@version" entries. A listed package is BLOCKed on sight — no graph
# query needed to know the answer — with the real blast-radius report
# (exposed services, shared maintainers, typosquats) attached to the
# decision. Empty/unset is the normal state; this is for an active incident.
# VIGIL_COMPROMISED_PACKAGES=""
#
# Populate code_graph with real npm dependency/maintainer/typosquat data
# (deps.dev + npm registry, not fixtures) before demoing blast-radius:
#   python3 scripts/ingest_npm.py --count 500

# ─── Detector thresholds (OPTIONAL) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# VIGIL_LOOP_MAX_REPEATS="5"
# VIGIL_RETRY_STORM_MAX="3"
# VIGIL_LATENCY_SPIKE="30s"
# VIGIL_TOOL_TIMEOUT="60s"
# VIGIL_AGENT_STUCK="120s"

# ─── SigNoz Cloud trace export (OPTIONAL) ───────────────────────────────────
# Unset: the server logs a warning and runs normally with export skipped.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT="https://ingest.<region>.signoz.cloud"
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS="signoz-ingestion-key=YOUR_KEY"

# ─── ClickHouse (OPTIONAL) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset: in-memory stores. Agent DNA baselines and prompt replay need this.
# VIGIL_CLICKHOUSE_DSN=""

# ─── Incident notifications (OPTIONAL) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Unset: governance alerts go to the dashboard over WebSocket only.
# VIGIL_WEBHOOK_URL=""
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# ─── Frontend ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set in frontend/.env.local, not here. Server-side only — no NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix, or the value ships to the browser.
# VIGIL_BACKEND_URL="http://localhost:8080"
#
# This one IS browser-side: Next rewrites cannot proxy WebSocket upgrades, so
# the dashboard needs the socket URL directly.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VIGIL_WS_URL="ws://localhost:8080/api/v1/vigil/ws"
